## Existing UI Audit & Stateful Macro Session Workflow

Existing UI audit or restyle (no design session): read `basecoat://design/theming` and `basecoat://design/rhythm`, call `get_component_details` or `basecoat_component_syntax` for each control you change, then run `basecoat_quality_inspector` (or `validate_composition`) on the rendered layout including `<head>` scripts and `<style>`. Select an official Basecoat style pack in CSS (default `@import "basecoat-css/vega"`, or `nova`, `maia`, `lyra`, `mira`, `luma`, `sera`, `rhea`).
Keep one dark-mode strategy (`html.dark`) and one resolver (`window.basecoat.theme`, key `themeMode`). For headers, pair `theme-switcher` (sun/moon SVG toggle) with the style variant selector. Verify light and dark screenshots with an OS dark preference and empty `localStorage`. Steps below are for stateful multi-step macro sessions:

1. Read `basecoat://design/rhythm` and `basecoat://project/context`. Read `basecoat://integration/frameworks` before production asset integration.
2. Identify the explicit design session. If unknown, call `get_design_context` with `view: "sessions"`. Never guess a global "current session".
3. Read the current context and revision with `get_design_context` (`overview` or `next`).
4. Select or resume an approved page or flow recipe such as `workspace-dashboard`, `workspace-settings`, `workspace-detail`, `auth-flow`, `auth-split-flow`, `marketing-pricing`, or `data-records`. Create the session with `begin_design`; change shared decisions with `apply_design_patch` using a `set_decision` operation.
5. Make structural changes through `apply_design_patch`, always using the current `expectedRevision` and a unique `operationId`. A successful commit acknowledges with revision and `operationId`; if the packet includes `truncated: true`, reread validation or context for omitted detail.
6. Before implementing a region, read its focused context, applicable `rules`, and referenced blueprint sections with `get_macro_block`. Follow `next` cursors until the required section is complete.
7. For semantic consistency, use `get_rhythm_rules` to access approved spacing, typography, and layout patterns. Filter by profile and family as needed. Keep each returned semantic token ID in design metadata and use its separate approved utility in application `class` attributes. A token ID is not itself a Tailwind class. Optional host overrides live at `.basecoat/rhythm.json` inside the project root only. Use `get_fsm_recipe` for structural states, transitions, guards, and metadata-only actions (available recipes: `dialog`, `collapsible-navigation`, `navigation`, `auth-flow`, `tabs`). Follow every `next` cursor from either tool. Bind source explicitly with matching `data-fsm-recipe` and `data-fsm-state` attributes.
8. Expand the approved static fragments in the host application. Preserve named slots, instance boundaries, ordering, and declared connections. The server does not write application source files. When writing HTML or framework template source, output literal line breaks rather than escaped `\n` strings. Prevent syntax bugs such as emitting raw `\n` characters in `<head>`.
9. Use `search_components` and `get_component_details` only for selected component leaves. Keep OAuth, captcha, session, credential, data, and other runtime behavior in the host application. Strictly enforce a neutral (zinc/slate) palette; forbid aggressive colors except for semantic (success/danger) indicators. Completely forbid emojis. Use inline SVG (e.g., Phosphor Icons) and avoid external CDN dependencies.
10. Record completed regions as client-reported checkpoints with `apply_design_patch` and a `record_written` operation tied to the current plan digest.
11. After a conflict or restart, reread context. Do not continue from an assumed stale layout. A changed registry or design revision invalidates old cursors.
12. Validate the complete graph with `validate_design` in `complete` mode. Compare generated source with that graph, then run `validate_composition` on the generated application source with the selected `semanticProfile` and `densityProfile` (e.g., `compact` for dense dashboards). Resolve parser diagnostics, invalid mappings, arbitrary colors, duplicate page landmarks, macro anchors, structural order, and FSM bindings.
13. Report remaining obligations explicitly. A graph marked `plan-ready` is not proof of rendered correctness, runtime behavior, accessibility, or visual verification, runtime interaction testing, or full framework AST verification. Perform and report those checks separately when required.

Layout contracts come from this project's pinned macro registry via MCP tools. They are not supplied automatically by shadcn or by MCP itself.
